<p><strong>The B2B Sales Approach:</strong></p>
<p>Commercial maintenance sales require a different playbook than residential. You&#39;re selling to facility managers, property owners, and operations directors who think in terms of total cost of ownership, not immediate comfort.</p>
<p><strong>Your pitch should focus on:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Cost avoidance:</strong> &quot;Our maintenance program reduces emergency repair costs by 40-60% and extends equipment life by 3-5 years.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Compliance:</strong> &quot;Regular maintenance keeps you compliant with warranty requirements and local building codes.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Business continuity:</strong> &quot;A failed system on a Saturday in July costs you more than a year of maintenance.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Predictable budgeting:</strong> &quot;Fixed monthly costs eliminate surprise repair bills.&quot;</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Marketing Channels:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>LinkedIn:</strong> The most effective B2B channel for HVAC. Post case studies showing energy savings and downtime reduction. Target facility managers, property managers, and business owners in your service area.</li>
<li><strong>Direct Sales:</strong> Assign a commercial sales rep to identify and prospect target accounts. Cold outreach works when it&#39;s personalized: &quot;I noticed your building is 15 years old. Most systems of that age need significant maintenance to avoid summer failures.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Email Drip Campaigns:</strong> Create a five-email sequence for commercial prospects covering cost of ownership, case studies, service differentiators, and a limited-time offer.</li>
<li><strong>Geofencing Ads:</strong> Target commercial zones, industrial parks, and competitor locations with mobile ads promoting your commercial maintenance program.</li>
</ul>
