<h2>Fall Strategies (September-November)</h2>
<p>Fall is the most underutilized marketing season in HVAC. Homeowners are recovering from summer expenses, kids are back in school, and the holiday spending season hasn&#39;t started yet. Smart contractors use this lull to book heating maintenance appointments before winter demand peaks — and to capture replacement sales from homeowners who don&#39;t want to risk a mid-winter furnace failure.</p>
<h3>Strategy 7: Launch a Heating System Inspection Campaign</h3>
<p><strong>The Strategy:</strong></p>
<p>A heating inspection campaign is the fall equivalent of your spring AC tune-up push. The goal is to get into as many homes as possible before the first cold snap, identify potential issues, and either fix them or schedule replacements before emergency calls flood your dispatch board.</p>
<p><strong>How to Execute:</strong></p>
<p>Start your heating inspection campaign in early September — before homeowners start thinking about winter. By the time the first frost hits, you want your maintenance schedule full and your replacement pipeline stocked.</p>
<p><strong>Offer Structure:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>&quot;Fall Furnace Check&quot; — $79-$129</strong> depending on market</li>
<li>Include: full system inspection, filter replacement, carbon monoxide test, thermostat calibration, and a written condition report</li>
<li>Bundle with a water heater inspection for an additional $29-$49</li>
<li>Add a &quot;winter readiness guarantee&quot; — if the system fails within 30 days of inspection, the diagnostic fee is waived</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The Carbon Monoxide Angle:</strong></p>
<p>Carbon monoxide safety is your most powerful fall marketing message. Every year, <strong>400+ Americans die</strong> from accidental CO poisoning, and thousands more are hospitalized. A furnace inspection that includes CO testing isn&#39;t a luxury — it&#39;s a safety essential. This framing elevates your service from &quot;maintenance&quot; to &quot;protection,&quot; which justifies the cost and creates emotional urgency.</p>
<p><strong>Marketing Messaging:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Is Your Furnace Safe? Free CO Test with Every Fall Inspection&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Don&#39;t Find Out the Hard Way — Get Your Furnace Checked Before the Cold&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Last Winter, 47% of Emergency Heating Calls Could Have Been Prevented&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Channel Strategy:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Email to past customers (50% of budget):</strong> Your highest-ROI channel. Send a three-email sequence with increasing urgency.</li>
<li><strong>Google LSAs and Search Ads (30% of budget):</strong> Target &quot;furnace inspection,&quot; &quot;heating system check,&quot; and &quot;fall HVAC maintenance.&quot;</li>
<li><strong>Social media (10% of budget):</strong> Facebook ads targeting homeowners 40+ with messaging about safety and preparedness.</li>
<li><strong>Direct mail (10% of budget):</strong> Postcards to homes 15+ years old with furnace replacement age statistics.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The Replacement Pipeline:</strong></p>
<p>Heating inspections are replacement goldmines. A technician who finds a cracked heat exchanger, corroded burner, or failing blower motor has a legitimate, urgent reason to recommend replacement. Document everything with photos and video. A homeowner who sees a photo of a rusted heat exchanger doesn&#39;t need much convincing to invest in a new furnace.</p>
<hr>
